 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to immunity from civil liability for emergency removal of a child from a motor vehicle",
    "basePrintNo": "S240",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S240-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 43,
       "fullName": "Kathleen A. Marchione",
       "imgName": "435_kathleen_a._marchione.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 435,
       "sessionMemberId": 699,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "MARCHIONE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §389, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S240\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to immunity from civil liability for emergency removal of a\nchild from a motor vehicle\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo eliminate the civil liability for damage resulting from forcible\nentry into a vehicle in certain cases.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one creates a new section, 389 to the Vehicle and Traffic Law\nrelating to civil immunity for forcible entry. This section states: a\nperson shall be immune from civil liability for any damage resulting\nfrom the forcible entry of a motor vehicle for the purpose of removing\na minor from said vehicle if the person determines prior that; the\nvehicle is locked and there is otherwise no reasonable method for the\nminor to exit the vehicle; has a good faith belief the minor is in\ndanger or suffering harm if not immediately removed from the vehicle;\nhas contacted local law enforcement, the fire department or a 911\noperator; places a notice on the windshield with their contact\ninformation and reason the entry was made; remains with the minor in a\nsafe location reasonably close to the vehicle until law enforcement\narrives; used no more force to enter the vehicle and remove the minor\nthan was necessary. For the purposes of this section a minor is a\nchild under the age of 8 years old. Section two sets the effective\ndate.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nEach year, parents or caregivers leave children unattended in a\nvehicle and the result can be injury or death. Last year, 44 children\nacross the nation died from vehicular heat stroke; as of July 29,\n2014, 16 children have died from vehicular heat stroke. This important\npiece of legislation will go a long way to help prevent these\nsenseless tragedies.\n\nAccording to SafeKids.org, even on a mild 70 degree day, the\ntemperature inside of a car can rise 19 degrees in 10 minutes and keep\ngetting hotter with each passing minute. The temperature can continue\nto climb much higher than it is outside, and cracking the windows\ndoesn't help. In addition, according to KidsandCars.org, children's\nbody temperatures increase 3-5 times faster than an adult.  Therefore,\nimmediate attention can make the difference between life and death for\na young child.\n\nThis legislation would allow an individual in New York State to\nlegally break a window or otherwise forcibly enter a parked car\nwithout liability if there is a good faith belief the actions would\nhelp a child under the age of eight years old whom would have\notherwise suffered harm if not immediately removed from the vehicle.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nS. 7930 (2014) FISCAL\n\n IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
